  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nHeight rises will reside over Iowa on Sunday, and the advection\nwing will have lifted into Minnesota. This will keep Sunday \ncloudy and cooler, but dry. The complex synoptic trough will \nincrease southwest flow Sunday night with rain beginning to \narrive. Its influence will keep precipitation chances going all \nthe way through at least Wednesday. The highest chance for \nthunderstorms looks to be Monday afternoon and evening. This is \nwhen the lower and upper level jet align parallel to the cold \nfront and the instability axis makes a run north. Severe weather\nis questionable at this range given the overnight and morning \nstorms; cloud cover from these will make surface \ndestabilization challenging. Beyond Monday, a northern stream \nwave will usher in another cold front from the northwest, \nreinforcing the wrap around precip from the first system. \nWidespread moderate rain is possible from the cumulative rounds \nof rain. Most of the state could see a total between 1 to 2 \ninches, but the rainfall will be spread in a large enough \ntime span that hydrology impacts will be minimal. A cooldown \nfollows the complex of waves with high temperatures returning to\nthe 50s by next weekend.",
